{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is complaining that Ludo King is rigged: they believe the game is designed to make them lose regardless of skill, giving opponents better dice rolls and themselves worse ones. This is a claim about concealed or manipulated determination of match outcomes, not a report of an unlucky run or a general difficulty complaint. The mention of glitches is a technical-fault complaint and does not itself suppress the rigging claim, which is described as a design behaviour rather than a malfunction. Playing against opponents is the setting in which the rigging is alleged to operate, so the competitive structure is part of what is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the opponents get good counts and you get small counts",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review places the player in direct opposition to other players, whose rolls are described as favoured over the reviewer's, so the game is described as making the player compete against others."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "if you are meant to lose, no matter how good you play you will definitley lose and in most of the cases this is the scene always..the opponents get good counts and you get small counts",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The span asserts that the game fixes who wins, making skill irrelevant, which is a claim about concealed determination of outcomes rather than a bad run."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a single, concrete in-game behaviour: an advertisement appears that has no working close control, so the game cannot proceed. The reviewer's only way forward is to quit and relaunch the app. The ad blocks continued play and cannot be dismissed, so the player is compelled to sit through or at least be stalled by the advertisement. There is no attribution to a bug or crash, and no other mechanic (currency, payment, social, temporal) is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Can't close the ads, therefore can't continue playing without closing and restarting the game.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The ad cannot be dismissed and blocks continuing play, so the player is compelled to deal with the advertisement before the game proceeds."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is a short positive appraisal: the reviewer praises the game's features and bonuses, then recounts a single incident in which they accidentally made a real-money purchase of a large coin pack and the developer refunded it promptly. Reading the incident for what it implies about the game's design: the game sells coin packs for real money, so the reviewer's own action evidences a real-money-to-in-game-currency purchase channel. There is no description of any interface mechanism that made the purchase easy, and the reviewer takes personal responsibility ('I messed up') while crediting the developer's refund, so the accident does not evidence absent confirmation or refund ability (M_EasyToPurchase's indicator requires the design to remove friction or confirmation). 'Generous bonuses' is a statement of reward value, not of a delivery mechanism, so it does not support Reward Mania. The one mechanic actually evidenced by the text is the sale of in-game coins for real money.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "accidentally bought a huge coin pack",
      "rule_applied": "definition; vs Purchases denominated in currency",
      "rationale": "The coins are the object acquired through a real-money purchase, so the span describes the conversion of money into in-game currency regardless of the reviewer's approving tone."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes two things: an unnamed microtransaction and ad strategy that ruins the game, and a specific behavior where after watching rewarded ads the game shows a fake 'can't connect' error so the promised reward is never granted. The microtransaction mention is too vague to code under any monetary label: no purchase mechanism, currency conversion, price framing, or progress gate is described. The ad behavior, however, describes the game's rewarded-ad mechanic: the player watches ads specifically to obtain rewards, and the review treats the connection failure as deliberate ('conveniently'), i.e., a design behavior of the installed game rather than an innocent technical fault. Since the reviewer frames the failure as a ruse rather than a genuine bug, R11 does not suppress coding. The ad-for-reward exchange is the Advertisement pattern: ads viewed under a promise of in-game compensation.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "when you watch the ads, it conveniently \"can't connect\" so you can't get rewards for the ads you watched, which defeats the whole purpose of an ad supported game",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Ads are the price paid to obtain in-game rewards, and the reviewer describes that reward exchange failing; the 'conveniently' wording attributes the failure to deliberate design rather than a genuine fault, so the incentivised ad-viewing mechanic is what is being described."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes Whiteout Survival as an idle base-builder game with standard systems (heroes, scouting other bases, gathering resources) and notes that during leveling the player must wait for building times. The only game-design behaviour actually described as present and time-related is the build timer wait. The closing suggestion for more mini games or rewards is a request for features that do not currently exist, so it is uncodable under R12. No payment, social, chance, or psychological manipulation is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "waiting for building times",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "Building timers are in-game timers that impose a wait on the player, and the reviewer explicitly describes waiting on them while leveling, which is the Wait to Play instance of Playing by Appointment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes Solitaire Grand Harvest's level-entry economy. Playing each level consumes credits, and the per-level cost has risen (2.8k per level) faster than the credits the player can earn from crops. Once credits run out, the only ways to keep playing are to wait an hour for the resource to come back or to pay cash for credits. So the review describes an energy-like resource that gates play on a real-time timer, with a paid shortcut that removes the wait.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "waiting an hour to play again",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "Running out of credits blocks play until an hour-long timer passes, so the player must wait on the game's schedule to continue."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "waiting an hour to play again or shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Skip",
      "rationale": "Spending cash on credits is presented as the alternative to waiting out the timer, so money bypasses a wait that blocks continued play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "vs Purchases denominated in currency",
      "rationale": "Real money is exchanged for the in-game credits, making the currency the object acquired and the conversion explicit in the span."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes losing a seven-day login streak three times, despite not missing days. A login streak is a daily-return incentive: the game rewards consecutive daily logins and resets that accumulated progress when a day is missed. The reviewer attributes the loss to an unexplained fault, but the underlying design rule is that the streak is fragile and is lost, imposing the penalty on the player. Per R11, a bug attribution does not suppress the label where the game's own rule—streak reset—caused the forfeit.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I've lost my 7 day streak for no reason whatsoever",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "A login streak that is lost when a day is missed is a daily incentive with a penalty attached; the reviewer describes the streak resetting through no action of their own, which the game's streak rule enforces."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a merge game where the player's activity is constrained by a limited pool of attempts that refill on a timer ('nearly 2 minutes for new chances to play'), and where daily missions demand more attempts than the player is given ('daily missions are impossible to do in such less attempts'). The daily missions are a resetting daily task system whose rewards the player forfeits when the attempts run out before completion. The final complaint about items not matching orders is too ambiguous (possible glitch or randomness of item production) to anchor any defined dark pattern, and 'fun in the starting stage'/'pathetic' are evaluations rather than mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it takes nearly 2 minutes for new chances to play",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "The span names an in-game refill timer that makes the player wait for more attempts before continuing, so play is gated by the game's clock rather than the player's choice."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "The daily missions are impossible to do in such less attempts",
      "rule_applied": "Daily task lists with resetting rewards",
      "rationale": "Daily missions are a resetting daily task system that pulls the player into daily sessions and whose rewards are lost when the day's limited attempts cannot complete them."
    }
  ],
